WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.149 --> 00:00:05.640
Steven Pinker, thanks for making time to meet
us here at Harvard, still the best university

00:00:05.640 --> 00:00:06.640
in the world.

00:00:06.640 --> 00:00:14.309
Can you tell us a little bit more about the
interest of your scholarly work?

00:00:14.309 --> 00:00:18.590
What is it you are working on, and why?

00:00:18.590 --> 00:00:23.449
I'm interested in human nature, and fortunately
that covers many topics.

00:00:23.449 --> 00:00:29.539
I have written on everything, from how to
write well to why languages have irregular

00:00:29.539 --> 00:00:38.540
verbs, to the historical trends in war, peace,
murder and genocide, to visual cognition:

00:00:38.540 --> 00:00:42.890
how we form mental images, how we recognize
shapes and faces.

00:00:42.890 --> 00:00:47.070
One of the great advantages of being a psychologist
is that you can write about all of these topics,

00:00:47.070 --> 00:00:49.710
because they all have to do with human nature.

00:00:49.710 --> 00:01:02.870
There are a few other fields - like philosophers,
or theologians - who also claim to know everything.

00:01:02.870 --> 00:01:05.199
What's the difference?

00:01:05.199 --> 00:01:10.260
Well, philosophy is very much related to psychology
- it was philosophers who first raised questions

00:01:10.260 --> 00:01:12.170
about human nature.

00:01:12.170 --> 00:01:17.690
I don't think there is a sharp division between
the field of philosophy and the field of psychology.

00:01:17.690 --> 00:01:22.500
There are philosophical questions and psychological
questions: philosophical questions about the

00:01:22.500 --> 00:01:26.960
nature of ideas, how they are logically related
to each other.

00:01:26.960 --> 00:01:32.860
Psychological questions are more empirical:
how does the human brain come to think certain

00:01:32.860 --> 00:01:36.470
kinds of thoughts and have certain kinds of
values?

00:01:36.470 --> 00:01:41.780
But in my experience they have all been closely
related.

00:01:41.780 --> 00:01:47.390
You have been extremely kind to accept our
invitation to be one of the speakers at the

00:01:47.390 --> 00:01:51.220
Nexus conference on November 12th: 'What Will
Save the World'.

00:01:51.220 --> 00:01:59.890
You are world-famous, you are very much in
demand, extremely busy; why did you accept

00:01:59.890 --> 00:02:05.430
the invitation to come to Amsterdam, to make
time and speak at that conference?

00:02:05.430 --> 00:02:10.140
I liked the question - there can be no bigger
question than 'what will save the world'.

00:02:10.140 --> 00:02:14.739
I don't think that anything will save the
world in the sense of bringing utopia to Earth,

00:02:14.739 --> 00:02:18.019
but I think the world can be improved, and
that will be the version of the question that

00:02:18.019 --> 00:02:19.780
I am very much interested in.

00:02:19.780 --> 00:02:24.630
I think the world has improved, and can improve
further.

00:02:24.630 --> 00:02:32.230
One of the most famous people in the world
of psychology, Sigmund Freud, was much less

00:02:32.230 --> 00:02:34.370
optimistic.

00:02:34.370 --> 00:02:40.969
If I understand him well, he was especially
interested in the question: 'Why are things

00:02:40.969 --> 00:02:42.639
going wrong all the time?'

00:02:42.639 --> 00:02:47.359
We will also have religious people at the
conference, and probably they will say it

00:02:47.359 --> 00:02:50.040
goes wrong because there is something like
original sin.

00:02:50.040 --> 00:02:55.120
On the secular level, Freud will point at
the dark side of human nature.

00:02:55.120 --> 00:03:00.969
Why do you think things go wrong so often?

00:03:00.969 --> 00:03:04.889
Well, like Freud I do believe that human nature
has a dark side.

00:03:04.889 --> 00:03:11.890
I believe that being a member of a civilized
society involves compromises and discontent,

00:03:11.890 --> 00:03:14.159
as Freud so famously put it.

00:03:14.159 --> 00:03:21.109
I think that aspects of human nature inevitably
come into conflict with aspects in other people.

00:03:21.109 --> 00:03:24.529
I don't think that men and women want exactly
the same thing, and I don't think that parents

00:03:24.529 --> 00:03:26.790
and children want exactly the same thing.

00:03:26.790 --> 00:03:28.900
Even close friends don't always want the same
thing.

00:03:28.900 --> 00:03:31.730
There is always tension, there are always
conflicts of interests.

00:03:31.730 --> 00:03:34.329
That is why there is great literature, that
is why there is tragedy.

00:03:34.329 --> 00:03:41.609
On the other hand, one component of human nature
is the ability to pose and solve problems,

00:03:41.609 --> 00:03:46.120
and to communicate our solutions to other
people via language.

00:03:46.120 --> 00:03:54.569
Thanks to writing we can accumulate good ideas,
we can try out ways of improving human life,

00:03:54.569 --> 00:03:58.389
keep the ones that work and discard the ones
that don't, learn from our mistakes, learn

00:03:58.389 --> 00:04:00.049
from our tragedies.

00:04:00.049 --> 00:04:04.629
And bit by bit, figure out ways in which we
can minimize the effects of human conflict.

00:04:04.629 --> 00:04:06.819
And I think the data show that we've been
doing it.

00:04:06.819 --> 00:04:13.349
Another reason that Freud was so pessimistic
was that he was situated in one of the worst

00:04:13.349 --> 00:04:19.449
times of human history, the years following
the First World War, which he lived through,

00:04:19.449 --> 00:04:23.620
and immediately preceding the Second World
War, he lived to see the 'Anschluss', his

00:04:23.620 --> 00:04:29.300
daughter was arrested by the Nazis, he escaped
just in time to spend the last year of his

00:04:29.300 --> 00:04:30.660
life in London.

00:04:30.660 --> 00:04:37.659
And so he saw the clouds gathering for one
of the worst interludes in human history,

00:04:37.659 --> 00:04:39.849
the Second World War.

00:04:39.849 --> 00:04:46.050
Indeed, if the world that Freud lived in had
continued, none of us should be an optimist,

00:04:46.050 --> 00:04:48.730
because those were dark days indeed.

00:04:48.730 --> 00:04:53.441
Of course, there were predictions after the
war that the two World Wars were just the

00:04:53.441 --> 00:04:57.720
beginning of an escalating series: World War
3 was inevitable, and it would be even worse

00:04:57.720 --> 00:05:00.300
because of nuclear weapons.

00:05:00.300 --> 00:05:04.909
We are now more than 70 years after the end
of World War 2 and World War 3 did not happen;

00:05:04.909 --> 00:05:08.389
no nuclear weapon has been used since Nagasaki.

00:05:08.389 --> 00:05:17.569
The world has become more democratic, more
affluent, more free, more peaceful in these

00:05:17.740 --> 00:05:18.740
70 years.

00:05:18.740 --> 00:05:22.810
That's no guarantee that it will continue,
no one can prophesy the future, I don't have

00:05:22.810 --> 00:05:26.289
the gift of clairvoyance.

00:05:26.289 --> 00:05:32.479
But it shows that the kind of doom that many
intellectuals felt after Word War 2 is not

00:05:32.479 --> 00:05:33.479
inevitable.

00:05:33.479 --> 00:05:39.179
There are actions that we can take that reduce
the risks.

